id: design.cache-aside
kind: design
title: Cache aside (read-through)
aliases: cache aside, read through, lazy caching, cache backed api
tags: caching, performance
questions:
  - how would you cache a database read?
invariant: The application reads the cache, falls back to the source of truth on a miss, and populates the cache with a TTL.
answer_30s: On read, check the cache, and on a miss load from the database and store the result with a TTL; invalidate on write rather than trying to update the cache.
answer_2m: |
  Cache-aside is the default because it is easy to reason about and degrades
  safely (a cold or unavailable cache just means more database load). The two
  rules that prevent most bugs: always set a TTL, so a missed invalidation is
  self-healing rather than permanent, and invalidate on write instead of
  updating, because an update is a second write that can succeed while the
  database write fails (or vice versa). Write-through (write to cache and
  database together) and write-behind (asynchronous flush) are the variants
  worth mentioning with their trade-offs: write-through costs latency on writes
  but keeps the cache hot, write-behind risks losing recent writes. Cache
  stampede is the classic failure: many callers miss the same key and all hit
  the database, fixed by single-flight/short-term locks or by refreshing hot
  keys proactively.
when_to_use:
  - read-heavy endpoints with a clear source of truth and tolerable staleness
avoid_when:
  - strong consistency requirements, or data that is written far more than read
minimal_example: |
  value = cache.get(key)
  if value is None:
      value = db.load(key)          # source of truth
      cache.set(key, value, ttl=60) # bound staleness
  return value

  # on write:
  db.write(key, new_value)
  cache.delete(key)                 # invalidate, do not update
failure_modes:
  - no TTL, so a stale entry lives until someone notices
  - updating the cache on write instead of invalidating, so the two stores drift
  - stampede on a hot key after eviction or deploy (cold cache)
follow_ups:
  - how do you keep the cache and database consistent?
related:
  - design.cache-invalidation
  - design.redis-cache
  - design.backpressure
  - recipe.cache-backed-api
sources:
  - https://redis.io/docs/manual/patterns/
